WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.780
Ancient rocks from Western Australia show water reached deep inside Earth more than 3 billion years ago. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 182. Looking back over the past week, three threads stand out from the research covered across recent episodes. Advanced optical imaging now maps metabolic activity inside individual immune cells drawn from routine blood samples. Large perspective studies link borderline anemia to higher all-cause mortality

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:58.800
in cohorts exceeding 500,000 people. Faster walking pace associates with lower cardiovascular and all-cause mortality in additional large UK biobank analyses. Immune cells release specialized pro-resolving mediators, such as resolvins, while inflammation remains active. The pathway clears debris and prevents harmful build-up in affected tissues. Boosting the pathway reduced inflammation markers and sped pain relief in the studies.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:28.620
Strategies that only block inflammatory signals may interfere with the body's own off switch. The discovery came from direct examination of immune cell behavior in both healthy and diseased states. Now shifting to early Earth processes. Western Australian rocks show water-rich crusts sank deep into the mantle more than 3 billion years ago. The process termed drip-duction, supplied water that triggered magma formation long before modern plate tectonics.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:43.940
Findings come from detailed analysis of specific ancient rock formations. Researchers propose that water-rich pieces of crust periodically sank into the mantle through drip-duction. The buried water then helped produce magma and volcanic eruptions.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:56.920
Climate simulations indicate the southern ocean could become a net carbon source even under declining emissions. The change appears in multiple net zero pathways modeled in the study published in science advances.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:12.420
Result challenges assumptions behind 1.5 degree Celsius mitigation plans that rely on continued ocean uptake. Researchers modeled multiple pathways to net zero conditions. The shift would occur even as overall emissions decline.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:27.760
New measurements question the assumption that only large phytoplankton efficiently export carbon to the deep sea. The study compared export efficiency across phytoplankton size classes and found small cyanobacteria contributes substantially.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:40.080
Results revise understanding of the biological carbon pumps operation. The biological carbon pump moves organic carbon from surface waters to the sea floor where it can remain for centuries.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:52.440
Cyanobacteria may play a larger role than previously thought. Base editing reaches every cell in human embryos, yet generates off-target genetic alterations difficult to forecast.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:03:02.780
Changes arise from interactions with the unique chromatin environment of early embryonic cells. The study examined editing outcomes across multiple embryos.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:13.380
Work supplies a controlled system for studying regulatory sequences while highlighting current safety limits. The technique gives tools for understanding normal human development.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:29.000
It also causes unpredictable genetic changes that currently preclude clinically use. The Columbia University embryo editing work shows that base editing can modify every cell, yet still produces off-target genetic changes that are difficult to predict.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:45.820
These changes arise because the editing machinery interacts with the unique chromatin environment of early embryonic cells. The result limits immediate clinical translation, but supplies a controlled system for studying how regulatory sequences function during the first cell divisions.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:04:00.460
Future experiments could test whether refined delivery methods or timing reduce those unpredictable edits. What specific safety thresholds would need to be met before such editing moves from research tools to any therapeutic consideration?

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:09.480
A team at the University of Malaga developed technology based on magnetic nanoparticles for diagnosing allergy to beta-lactam antibiotics.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:22.240
The method enables precise, rapid and reliable detection of reactions to a moxicillin and other penicillins. The multidisciplinary group combined nanoparticle engineering with clinical sample analysis.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.880
This approach targets allergies that affect many patients prescribed common antibiotics.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:37.820
Reduced saliva production affects cancer patients after head and neck radiation, people with shiogren syndrome, those with diabetes, and older adults.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:49.600
Researchers point to hidden cell stress as the underlying issue. Dry mouth leads to difficulty eating, speaking, swallowing, altered taste, and higher risk of dental cavities and infections.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:58.720
The work examined cellular responses across these different patient groups, identifying the stress mechanism opens routes to targeted interventions.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:07.440
Physicists at CERN used the Alice experiment to measure particle production at unprecedented spatial resolution inside atomic nuclei.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:20.720
They distinguished between competing explanations for glue on behavior. At scales about one quarter the size of a proton, J-slash-py production dropped in a way conventional nuclear shadowing struggles to explain.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:28.300
The measurements provide new data on structures inside protons. The work was conducted at the Large Hadron Collider.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:37.880
Researchers at the University of British Columbia found that shrinking coastal wetlands from rising seas could put shorebirds and their critical habitat at risk.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:50.520
Wetlands around the world are expected to contract as oceans rise. The study modeled habitat loss across multiple coastal regions, shorebirds rely on these areas for feeding and breeding during migration.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.020
The findings link sea level projections directly to population level risks for these species.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:04.200
A 10-year study of more than 6,000 adults found coronary calcium scans may add little to standard risk estimates from many people.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.960
For patients with borderline or intermediate risk, the scans could reveal who is more likely to develop heart disease.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:15.480
The work tracked outcomes across a large cohort over the full decade.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.700
Standard estimates already incorporate traditional factors such as age and cholesterol.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:33.600
The additional imaging information proved most useful in the intermediate risk subgroup. Harvard researchers demonstrated protection of quantum information using mechanical vibrations around a diamond-based qubit.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:43.720
The approach extended coherence time by roughly threefold. The same phonons could eventually transmit and protect quantum information in compact on-chip networks.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:52.660
The vibrations continuously surround the qubit to reduce decoherence. This method offers a route toward integrated sound-based quantum devices.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:07:08.100
A new bioinformatics approach applied to total RNA-seq data from human brain tissue identified 3,022 recursive splice sites across 2,775 introns in 2,407 genes.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:17.180
Most sites occur in protein-coding genes. The median length of these recursively spliced introns is 10,114 base pairs.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:27.180
The method uses alignment to short-customize target sequences. This work substantially increases the catalog of known recursive splice sites in the human genome.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:36.320
Humanized Dressophila, expressing the APOE-4 variant, exhibited reduced lifespan when exposed to wrote-known or an obi-sogenic diet.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:43.320
The flies developed age-related memory loss and chemosensory deficits matching Alzheimer's-associated phenotypes.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:50.380
The model supports a multi-hit view of disease pathogenesis involving oxidative stress and lipid metabolism.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:08:01.680
APOE-3 expressing flies did not show the same reductions under identical conditions. The findings establish a scalable system for testing APOE-dependent mechanisms.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:08.360
Phoramoniferous fossils show repeated reversals in shell-coiling direction across geological time.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:14.000
The pattern may reflect an evolutionary process that plays out repeatedly across the planet.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:24.000
The reversals occur on time scales of a few thousand years. Researchers continue to examine what drives the consistent directional changes in these marine organisms.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:31.480
Before we go, keep an eye on further modeling of Southern Ocean carbon pathways in upcoming net-zero scenarios.

